074 "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/13/13d6206f24dffc537422873c012ff5400ce6cde163e8e48a1c057736408299fd.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,\\{2\\},\\{5\\},\\{7\\},\\{2,5\\},\\{2,7\\},\\{5,7\\},\\{2,5,7\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560976 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $S=\\{j,l\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ffb078f-cf87-5939-b390-ec244c755738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560976 #edb/ref 1074 "To find every subset of $S,$ we list out every combination of elements within $S.$\n\n We can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/a7/a741ce494203603a7a714b09f10f06cc32c281999e3c1a31178a2449945c88be.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,\\{j\\},\\{l\\},\\{j,l\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105037" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561007 #edb/ref 1071 "Which statement is **not** true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68f004d9-140c-5382-893d-19e68749e384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561007 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only $R⊆Q$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every real number is a rational number. For example, we have $\\sqrt{2}\\in R$ but $\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561012 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A=\\{x\\in N:x<5\\}$ and $B=\\{x\\in N:2x\\ge 4\\}?$\n\nI. $A⊆B$\n\nII. $B⊆A$\n\nIII. $∅⊆B$\n\nIV. $B⊇A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "713562a6-f85e-5713-a528-f52fb32799ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561012 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers less than $5.$ So,\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $2x\\ge 4,$ that is to say $x\\ge 2.$ So,\n\n$$\nB=\\{2,3,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $1\\in A$ but $1\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is false. For example, $5\\in B$ but $5\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅=\\{\\}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561020 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A=\\{x\\in Z∣-1\\le x+2\\le 3\\}$ and $B=\\{x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}<2\\}?$\n\nI. $A⊆B$\n\nII. $B⊆A$\n\nIII. $B⊈A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83ab9b35-c1e3-561b-9f67-abfc0e8e8a12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561020 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+2\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-3\\le x\\le 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{-3,-2,-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}<2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=\\{-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $-3\\in A$ but $-3\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Indeed, every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561044 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561044 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1207" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561044 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561044 #edb/ref 1071 "Which statement is **not** true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561044 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbde3d52-89b7-557b-8e4a-7c74e15e7a66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561044 #edb/ref 1074 "If $A⊆B,$ then $A$ is a subset of $B,$ meaning that every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nAmong the given choices, only $Z⊆N$ is not true. This statement is not true because not every integer is a natural number. For example, we have $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
